Some websites keep the content in a repository of its own. The history, the size and the access rights of the content are then separate from the code. You mount the content repository in the website as a Git submodule. Mica supports this since 0.11.0.

Mica works in the repository that holds the content root. It never commits in the website repository.

## Set it up

Add the content repository as a submodule. The submodule folder is the content root:

```bash
git submodule add https://github.com/acme/website-content.git content
git commit -m "Add the content as a submodule"
```

The model says where the content is, as before:

```ts
export const site = defineSite({ root: "./content", collections: { /* … */ } });
```

The Astro build writes `.mica/schema.json` into the content root, so into the submodule. Commit and push it in the submodule. Then commit the new submodule pointer in the website repository:

```bash
git -C content add .mica/schema.json
git -C content commit -m "Update the model"
git -C content push
git add content && git commit -m "Use the new model"
```

For the self-hosted editor, the content must be in a folder of the content repository, such as `content/` inside it. Mica refuses a content repository whose top is the content root. Then the submodule is at `site-content`, and the model has `root: "./site-content/content"`.

## The local editor

Run `npx @micacms/editor start` as usual. Save, Publish and the push go to the content repository, because Mica finds the repository with `git rev-parse --show-toplevel` in the content root.

A submodule is usually on a detached HEAD after `git submodule update`. A commit on a detached HEAD is on no branch, so no push can send it, and the next `git submodule update` hides it. So Mica does not publish then. The start banner and the Publish page give the command that fixes it:

```bash
git -C content switch main
```

After that, Publish works. Your drafts stay drafts until then.

## The website build

Mica never changes the submodule pointer in the website repository. The build gets the newest content itself:

```bash
git submodule update --init --remote content
```

In GitHub Actions, `actions/checkout` with `submodules: true` checks out the pointer of the website commit. Add the `--remote` update for the newest content:

```yaml
- uses: actions/checkout@v4
  with:
    submodules: true
    lfs: true   # only when the content uses Git LFS
- run: git submodule update --init --remote content
```

A publish pushes to the content repository, so the website does not build by itself. Mica adds no build webhook. Let a workflow in the content repository start the website build, or build the website on a schedule.

## The self-hosted editor

- `MICA_REPOSITORY_URL` is the URL of the content repository, not of the website.
- The content must be in a folder of that repository. Set `MICA_CONTENT_PATH` if there is more than one content root.
- Mica clones the content repository only. It never fetches submodules.
- A model change needs two commits: the `.mica/schema.json` in the content repository, and the new pointer in the website. The editor shows the old model until the schema is in the content repository.

## Git LFS

Git LFS works in the content repository. See [Large files with Git LFS](/docs/git-lfs/).

